# =============================================================================
# sandbox.ogs - a small city, explained line by line.
#
# This file is a ruleset. It holds no map and no building: it says what a
# building *is*, what it can hold, and what it does. The map lives next to it,
# in sandbox.ogc. Open one and you get the other.
#
# Read this file from top to bottom. Each block is explained before it starts.
#
# HOW TO READ THE SYNTAX
#
#   - A block has a name and is closed by the word "end".
#   - A "#" starts a comment. The rest of the line is ignored.
#   - Words are bare: no quotes, no commas, no semicolon.
#   - The order of the blocks does not matter to the engine. The order here is
#     the order that is easiest to read.
#   - Names are yours to choose. "People" and "Home" mean nothing to the
#     engine; it only checks that the same word is used everywhere.
#
# WHAT THE CITY DOES, IN ONE PARAGRAPH
#
#   Houses hold people. In the morning they send them to the factories, which
#   turn people into goods and into pollution. Trucks carry the goods to the
#   shops and to the restaurants. At noon the workers go for lunch. In the
#   afternoon the residents left at home go shopping, which is what makes
#   money. In the evening everybody goes home. Pollution lowers the value of a
#   district, and a district of low value loses its houses.
#
# IF YOU EDIT THIS FILE
#
#   A save records a fingerprint of the ruleset it was written against, and
#   refuses to open against a ruleset that changed since. Use Apply in the
#   Script panel of the demo: it reloads the rules and stamps the saves next
#   to this file with the new fingerprint.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# RESOURCES - the quantities the city counts.
#
# A resource is only a name. Nothing here says who holds it or how much.
# Buildings hold some, layers hold some, agents carry some, and the city holds
# some. Everything below refers to these names, so this block comes first.
#
# Think of them as the units of the game: not "twelve" but "twelve People".
# =============================================================================
resources
	resource Water
	resource Grass
	resource People
	resource Money
	resource Goods
	resource Power
	resource Pollution
	resource Trash
	resource Happiness
end


# =============================================================================
# PATHS AND SEGMENTS - the network the agents travel on.
#
# A "path" is a family of networks: roads here, but rails or water pipes would
# be other paths. Two paths never meet, so an agent that starts on a road stays
# on the road.
#
# A road drawn over another is cut, and the two meet at a crossroads a driver
# can turn at. That is what "crossings" says, and it is true unless you write
# otherwise. Add "crossings false" on a network of pipes or cables, where one
# line passing over another means nothing, and put the junctions you do want
# there by hand with the Nodes tool.
#
# A "segment" is one kind of connection between two crossroads:
#
#   speed      how fast an agent goes on it when it is empty.
#   capacity   how much traffic it takes before it slows down.
#   beta       how sharply it slows down once it is above that capacity.
#
# These three numbers are what the router uses to choose an itinerary. It does
# not look for the shortest road, but for the fastest one *now*: a short busy
# street can cost more than a long empty one.
# =============================================================================
paths
	path Road color 0xAAAAAA
end

segments
	segment Dirt color 0xAAAAAA speed 30 capacity 20 beta 4
end


# =============================================================================
# AGENTS - who travels, and how fast.
#
# An agent is sent by a building, with a load of resources. It looks for a
# building that answers to the name it was given, goes there, and gives the
# load away. Then it disappears.
#
# The engine does not know what a "Worker" is. The five agents below differ
# only by their colour and their speed. What makes one a worker and another a
# truck is the rule that sends it and the load it carries.
# =============================================================================
agents
	agent People color 0xFFFF00 speed 10
	agent Worker color 0xFFFFFF speed 10
	agent Shopper color 0xFFAA00 speed 12
	agent Diner color 0xFF6644 speed 12
	agent Truck color 0x8888FF speed 8
end


# =============================================================================
# RULES - everything the city does.
#
# This is the long block, and the interesting one. A rule is a list of
# conditions and of changes, all on the same footing.
#
# THE ONE THING TO REMEMBER: a rule is all or nothing. The engine reads every
# line first. If a single one refuses, nothing happens at all, and the rule is
# tried again later. So a house cannot send a worker it does not have, and a
# shop cannot sell goods it never received.
#
# There are three kinds of rule, and each one runs on a different thing:
#
#   buildingRule   runs on one building.
#   layerRule      runs on the cells of one layer.
#   zoneRule       runs on one rectangle painted by the player.
#
# A rule written here does nothing yet. It has to be listed in the "rules [ ]"
# of a building, of a layer or of a zone, further down the file. That is what
# attaches it to something.
#
# THE LINES YOU WILL SEE
#
#   rate 30 minutes        how often the rule is tried. Game time, not real
#                          time. A bare number would count engine ticks, and
#                          twenty ticks make one game minute, which says
#                          nothing to a reader. Write the unit on the same
#                          line as the number.
#
#   hour between 8 18      only between 8:00 and 18:00 on the city clock. If
#                          the first hour is the larger one, the range crosses
#                          midnight.
#
#   local People greater 0    a condition on the stock of *this* building.
#   local People remove 1     take one from it.
#   global Money add 1        the purse of the whole city.
#   layer Pollution add 1     the ground around the building. How far around
#                             is the "layerRadius" of the building.
#
#   agent Worker to Work add [ People 1 ]
#                          send a Worker carrying one People, looking for a
#                          building that answers to the name "Work".
#
# Note that "add" and "remove" are changes, while "greater" and "less" are
# conditions. Both are checked before anything runs.
# =============================================================================
rules

	# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# The ground. Layer rules run on cells, not on buildings.
	# -------------------------------------------------------------------------

	# Water slowly becomes grass. The interesting word is randomTilesPercent:
	# the rule visits only nine cells out of ten, drawn at random, instead of
	# every cell. Over time the result is the same as visiting all of them, and
	# it costs less on a large map. It also looks better: the grass does not
	# appear everywhere at once.
	layerRule CreateGrass
		rate 20 minutes
		layer Water remove 10 randomTilesPercent 90
		layer Grass add 1
	end

	# Pollution fades faster than it spreads on its own: what fouls a
	# neighbourhood is the factories, not the passing of time. Compare the two
	# periods below: 30 minutes to gain one, 15 minutes to lose one.
	layerRule SpreadPollution
		rate 30 minutes
		layer Pollution add 1
	end

	layerRule CleanPollution
		rate 15 minutes
		layer Pollution remove 1
	end

	# Desirability says how good an address is. It is the slow variable of the
	# city. It used to move every ten ticks, half a game minute, so a district
	# was built and abandoned within the hour. It now takes days to turn a good
	# address into a bad one, which is the speed a player can follow.
	layerRule DropDesirability
		rate 2 hours
		layer Pollution greater 20
		layer Desirability remove 2
	end

	layerRule RaiseDesirability
		rate 2 hours
		layer Pollution less 5
		layer Desirability add 1
	end

	# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# The day of a house.
	# -------------------------------------------------------------------------

	# What shares a household between the factories and the shops is the period
	# of the rules and the number of residents, not a condition on the house:
	# one leaves for work every three quarters of an hour here, against one
	# every three hours in ShopForGoods. Sending one out every twenty minutes
	# emptied the houses long before the shops opened, and every agent the city
	# ever made was a worker.
	#
	# A period runs on the clock of its own building, from a random phase, so a
	# house grown at noon starts its commute within the hour instead of waiting
	# for the next morning.
	buildingRule SendPeopleToWork
		rate 45 minutes
		hour between 8 18
		local People greater 0
		local People remove 1
		agent Worker to Work add [ People 1 ]
	end

	buildingRule SendPeopleToHome
		rate 20 minutes
		hour between 18 22
		local People remove 1
		agent People to Home add [ People 1 ]
	end

	# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# The day of a factory.
	# -------------------------------------------------------------------------

	# One worker present, one more item of goods, and some dirt on the ground
	# around. This single rule is where money enters the city.
	buildingRule ProduceGoods
		rate 30 minutes
		hour between 8 18
		local People greater 0
		local Goods add 1
		layer Pollution add 1
		global Money add 1
	end

	# The goods leave the factory on their own wheels. Without this the shops
	# never had anything to sell, and the workers looked like they were carrying
	# the stock home in the evening.
	buildingRule ShipGoods
		rate 45 minutes
		local Goods remove 1
		agent Truck to Shop add [ Goods 1 ]
	end

	# The restaurants are supplied by name. They used to answer to Shop as well,
	# which is how a truck bound for a shop, and a resident going shopping,
	# ended their trip at the nearest canteen instead. Two rules, two names, and
	# each truck knows where it is going.
	buildingRule ShipFood
		rate 2 hours
		local Goods remove 1
		agent Truck to Restaurant add [ Goods 1 ]
	end

	buildingRule GoEating
		rate 30 minutes
		hour between 12 14
		local People greater 0
		local People remove 1
		agent Diner to Restaurant add [ People 1 ]
	end

	# An example of a building writing on the ground rather than trading: the
	# factory waters the cells around it. Change layerRadius on the building and
	# you change how wide the effect is.
	buildingRule UsePeopleToWater
		rate 1 hour
		local People greater 1
		layer Water add 30
	end

	# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# The day of a shop.
	# -------------------------------------------------------------------------

	buildingRule ShopForGoods
		rate 3 hours
		hour between 10 20
		local People greater 0
		global Money greater 0
		local People remove 1
		agent Shopper to Shop add [ People 1 ]
	end

	# A shop sells to the customers standing in it. Without the first line it
	# turned its stock into money on its own, and the shoppers were decoration.
	# This is the line that makes money follow traffic.
	buildingRule SellGoods
		rate 20 minutes
		local People greater 0
		local Goods remove 1
		global Money add 2
		local Happiness add 1
	end

	buildingRule ServeLunch
		rate 15 minutes
		local People greater 0
		local Goods remove 1
		local Happiness add 1
	end

	# Nobody stays where they arrived. Each building that receives people sends
	# them on: the restaurant back to work, the shop back home. Remove these two
	# rules and the city fills up with people stuck in a shop.
	buildingRule SendDinersBack
		rate 20 minutes
		hour between 13 15
		local People greater 0
		local People remove 1
		agent Worker to Work add [ People 1 ]
	end

	buildingRule ReceiveShopper
		rate 30 minutes
		local People greater 0
		local People remove 1
		agent People to Home add [ People 1 ]
	end

	# Nothing adds Trash yet. The rule is here so that you have something to
	# start from: add "layer Trash add 1" to SellGoods and the shops will begin
	# to dirty their street.
	buildingRule CollectTrash
		rate 1 hour
		layer Trash remove 2
	end

	# -------------------------------------------------------------------------
	# Growth. Zone rules run on the rectangle the player painted.
	#
	#   count Home less 10          how many Home buildings the rectangle
	#                               already holds.
	#   spawn Home at nearestSegment    build one against the closest road.
	#   destroy Home                remove one.
	#
	# A zone with no road in reach grows nothing: a building needs a street to
	# stand on, or no agent could ever reach it.
	# -------------------------------------------------------------------------

	zoneRule GrowHomes
		rate 4 hours
		count Home less 10
		spawn Home at nearestSegment
	end

	zoneRule GrowShops
		rate 6 hours
		count Shop less 3
		spawn Shop at nearestSegment
	end

	zoneRule GrowRestaurants
		rate 12 hours
		count Restaurant less 2
		spawn Restaurant at nearestSegment
	end

	zoneRule GrowWorks
		rate 8 hours
		count Work less 3
		spawn Work at nearestSegment
	end

	# The only rule that takes something down. Note the second condition: the
	# zone keeps two houses whatever happens, so a district never disappears
	# completely and can come back once the air is clean again.
	zoneRule AbandonHomes
		rate 1 day
		layer Desirability less 2
		count Home greater 2
		destroy Home
	end
end


# =============================================================================
# BUILDINGS - the four kinds of building, and what each one is able to do.
#
# One line per kind. The parts of a line:
#
#   layerRadius 3      how far, in cells, its rules read and write the ground.
#                      The shape is a diamond around the building. A radius of
#                      1 is the cell itself and its four neighbours.
#
#   rules [ ... ]      the rules it runs. A rule missing from this list never
#                      runs on this building, however well it is written.
#
#   targets [ Work ]   the name agents look for. "agent Worker to Work" ends
#                      its trip in a building whose targets hold "Work". A
#                      building can answer to several names.
#
#   caps [ People 8 ]  how much it can hold. An agent carrying one more People
#                      to a full house is refused and keeps looking.
#
#   resources [ ... ]  what it holds when it is built. Houses start with eight
#                      residents; everything else starts empty and has to be
#                      supplied.
#
# Compare Work and Home below: the factory has a radius of 3 because it fouls a
# whole neighbourhood, the house has a radius of 1 because it does almost
# nothing to the ground.
# =============================================================================
buildings
	building Home color 0xFF00FF layerRadius 1 rules [ SendPeopleToWork ShopForGoods ] targets [ Home ] caps [ People 8 Happiness 10 ] resources [ People 8 ]
	building Work color 0x00AAFF layerRadius 3 rules [ SendPeopleToHome ProduceGoods ShipGoods ShipFood GoEating UsePeopleToWater ] targets [ Work ] caps [ People 12 Goods 20 ] resources [ ]
	building Shop color 0xFFAA00 layerRadius 2 rules [ SellGoods ReceiveShopper CollectTrash ] targets [ Shop ] caps [ People 6 Goods 30 Happiness 20 ] resources [ ]
	building Restaurant color 0xFF6644 layerRadius 1 rules [ ServeLunch SendDinersBack ] targets [ Restaurant ] caps [ People 8 Goods 20 Happiness 20 ] resources [ ]
end


# =============================================================================
# LAYERS - the ground under the city.
#
# A layer is one number per cell of the map, over the whole world. "capacity"
# is the most one cell can hold: what a rule adds above it is dropped, so
# raising the capacity of Pollution makes a dirty district dirtier and slower
# to clean. The demo draws each layer as a heatmap in the colour given here,
# and a cell at capacity is a cell drawn full.
#
# Water and Grass are the landscape. Pollution and Desirability are the two
# that drive the game: the factories raise the first, and the first lowers the
# second, which in the end empties the houses.
#
# Trash and Power hold no rule at all. They are drawn, they can be painted by
# hand with the Layers tool, and nothing reads them yet. They are there for you
# to use.
# =============================================================================
layers
	layer Water color 0x0000FF capacity 100 rules [  ]
	layer Grass color 0x00FF00 capacity 10 rules [ CreateGrass ]
	layer Pollution color 0x555555 capacity 80 rules [ SpreadPollution CleanPollution ]
	layer Desirability color 0x88FF88 capacity 50 rules [ DropDesirability RaiseDesirability ]
	layer Trash color 0x886644 capacity 40 rules [  ]
	layer Power color 0xFFFF66 capacity 100 rules [  ]
end


# =============================================================================
# ZONES - what the player paints on the map.
#
# A zone holds no building of its own. It is a rectangle plus a list of rules,
# and those rules are what build and remove things inside it. This is the whole
# of the RCI game: paint Residential next to a road, wait, and houses appear.
#
# Only Residential can lose its buildings, through AbandonHomes. Give the other
# two a rule of the same shape and a badly placed district will empty as well.
# =============================================================================
zones
	zone Residential color 0x44AA44 rules [ GrowHomes AbandonHomes ]
	zone Commercial color 0xDDAA44 rules [ GrowShops GrowRestaurants ]
	zone Industrial color 0xAA6644 rules [ GrowWorks ]
end
